{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The protocol assumes a durable epoch-evidence binding and tombstone survive the crash, yet the problem states the process leaves no terminal record. If evidence was completed in the workspace but the crash prevented the durable ledger from recording the (epoch, content_hash) pair, a restarted process sees only a workspace epoch tag with no matching evidence hash. The epoch-mismatch trigger therefore cannot distinguish completed evidence from an interrupted run, allowing silent displacement precisely when the terminal record is absent.","target":"213f658f3785db05d3b2fe53de668eb7ab50011449a96ccd9ce2f7be951148b9"},{"attack":true,"case":"By gating reclamation strictly on visibility of an external seal ledger, the protocol cannot handle the stated scenario where the process crashed before writing that seal. An absent seal means the protocol must either reclaim and silently displace workspace-resident completed evidence, or refuse to reclaim and fail to function as a reclamation protocol. It thus delegates safety to a separate ledger that the crashed process may not have updated, violating the requirement to never silently displace evidence in the no-terminal-record case.","target":"491f1422ce2e249132473b9fac078025a2a5d95c6e79d6d15e0b4526ab367af0"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"e51f3c9555120a6ced5092ff3539d4d80b80eeb61fe56ccb74b6be52df719791"}]}